WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.429 --> 00:00:05.240
As the post-credits scene in Avengers: Infinity
War and the final few minutes of her recently

00:00:05.240 --> 00:00:10.550
released solo movie have proven, Captain Marvel
is like the Kim Possible of the Marvel Cinematic

00:00:10.550 --> 00:00:13.120
Universe: call her, beep her, if you want
to reach her.

00:00:13.510 --> 00:00:15.700
Major Marvel fans know the story: S.H.I.E.L.D.

00:00:15.700 --> 00:00:20.990
agent Nick Fury paged Captain Marvel at the
tail end of Infinity War after Thanos snapped

00:00:20.990 --> 00:00:25.680
his Infinity Stone-powered fingers, and killed
half of all life in the universe.

00:00:26.130 --> 00:00:31.360
In Captain Marvel, set many years earlier,
Carol Danvers hands Fury his pager back before

00:00:31.360 --> 00:00:36.930
she leaves Earth to help the Skrulls locate
a new home world, telling him she made extra-special

00:00:36.930 --> 00:00:41.940
enhancements to his trusty communications
device and to only use it to contact her when

00:00:41.940 --> 00:00:44.580
there's a, quote, "real emergency" happening.

00:00:45.120 --> 00:00:48.480
Thanos decimating the universe is certainly
a real emergency!

00:00:48.920 --> 00:00:50.020
No one denies this.

00:00:50.520 --> 00:00:53.860
But what about all the other crises that have
gone down in the MCU?

00:00:54.180 --> 00:00:56.680
Did they not necessitate a call to Captain
Marvel?

00:00:57.000 --> 00:01:01.100
Exactly why did Fury wait until Infinity War
to page her?

00:01:01.100 --> 00:01:03.160
We finally know the answers to all those questions.

00:01:03.540 --> 00:01:04.040
Sort of.

00:01:04.360 --> 00:01:08.920
Movie news site SlashFilm did the world a
favor when they asked Marvel Studios president

00:01:08.930 --> 00:01:14.020
Kevin Feige in a recent interview why Fury
didn't contact Captain Marvel sooner — like,

00:01:14.020 --> 00:01:18.600
say, when that gigantic wormhole barfed up
a bunch of aliens over Manhattan in the original

00:01:18.600 --> 00:01:19.100
Avengers.

00:01:20.040 --> 00:01:24.640
That event was so bad that the World Security
Council launched a nuclear bomb at the city.

00:01:25.040 --> 00:01:30.300
Nick Fury had to run outside and fire an actual
bazooka at one of SHIELD's own planes to try

00:01:30.300 --> 00:01:31.060
and stop it!

00:01:31.300 --> 00:01:32.420
That's some rough stuff!

00:01:32.800 --> 00:01:37.320
If Captain Marvel is the most powerful character
in the MCU, and if she's apparently strong

00:01:37.330 --> 00:01:41.420
enough to help take down Thanos, why hasn't
she been spoken about in connection with the

00:01:41.420 --> 00:01:42.680
Avengers until now?

00:01:42.920 --> 00:01:44.080
Why hasn't she helped them out?

00:01:44.700 --> 00:01:46.960
According to Feige, there are two explanations.

00:01:47.450 --> 00:01:51.780
The first one is that Fury may not have considered
anything that went down before Infinity War

00:01:51.780 --> 00:01:55.400
a genuine emergency based on the scale of
the chaos.

00:01:55.600 --> 00:01:57.000
Aliens invading Manhattan?

00:01:57.320 --> 00:01:57.820
Who cares?

00:01:58.060 --> 00:01:59.240
It's just one city, right?

00:01:59.240 --> 00:01:59.880
No big deal.

00:02:00.369 --> 00:02:02.740
Killer robots threatening total world domination?

00:02:02.740 --> 00:02:06.310
Psh, whatever, that sounds like something
Tony Stark should be able to fix with a quick

00:02:06.310 --> 00:02:07.320
call to his IT guy.

00:02:10.460 --> 00:02:11.460
"Hello, IT?

00:02:12.460 --> 00:02:13.920
Have you tried turning it off and on again?"

00:02:15.780 --> 00:02:20.659
Nick Fury actor Samuel L. Jackson backed Feige
on this particular explanation, chatting with

00:02:20.660 --> 00:02:23.760
IGN about why Fury hasn't called Captain Marvel
before.

00:02:23.880 --> 00:02:28.420
"We finally have an emergency that I feel
warrants her presence.

00:02:28.720 --> 00:02:31.120
This is unprecedented.

00:02:31.120 --> 00:02:33.120
How are you gonna fight that?"

00:02:33.380 --> 00:02:36.400
Feige's second explanation is a little...hairier.

00:02:36.640 --> 00:02:40.580
According to the interview, Feige discussed
Fury's super-pager, saying:

00:02:40.960 --> 00:02:42.160
"[...] how do you know he never hit it?

00:02:42.560 --> 00:02:44.460
How do we know he never pushed it before?

00:02:44.940 --> 00:02:46.560
We've never seen him push it before.

00:02:46.860 --> 00:02:47.980
That doesn't mean he never did."

00:02:48.720 --> 00:02:50.540
Okay, maybe that's true?

00:02:50.920 --> 00:02:55.140
We never saw him page her...but there's a
lot of things we never saw Nick Fury do.

00:02:55.560 --> 00:03:00.519
We never saw him get eaten by a shark, but
it doesn't really make sense to accept that,

00:03:00.520 --> 00:03:01.520
well, perhaps he did.

00:03:02.089 --> 00:03:06.040
Anyway, it doesn't make too much sense to
speculate about the ins and outs of why Fury

00:03:06.040 --> 00:03:10.060
may or may not have called his BFF Captain
Marvel over all these years.

00:03:10.360 --> 00:03:14.819
That's because Avengers Endgame, which will
fly into theaters next month, will probably

00:03:14.819 --> 00:03:18.840
address Captain Marvel's whereabouts in the
time between her solo film and the end of

00:03:18.840 --> 00:03:19.600
Infinity War.

00:03:20.380 --> 00:03:23.769
Not only that, but it should also explain
why the heck she didn't rush to the aid of

00:03:23.769 --> 00:03:26.760
Earth's Mightiest Heroes when they weren't
feeling all that mighty.

00:03:26.960 --> 00:03:31.440
And hey, maybe she'll show off some of the
pages Nick Fury did send her over all those

00:03:31.440 --> 00:03:31.940
years.

00:03:32.280 --> 00:03:35.940
Maybe he kept her up-to-date on those cool
Star Wars prequels she was missing while she

00:03:35.940 --> 00:03:37.180
was flying around in space.

00:03:37.700 --> 00:03:41.020
"Did Samuel L. Jackson ask for a purple lightsaber?

00:03:41.260 --> 00:03:42.660
You bet your ass I did."

00:03:42.800 --> 00:03:46.060
Avengers: Endgame opens in theaters on April
26.

00:03:46.420 --> 00:03:50.860
Perhaps before then, Marvel will release a
montage of all the times Fury tried and failed

00:03:50.860 --> 00:03:53.560
to get Captain Marvel to swoop in to save
the day.

00:03:53.880 --> 00:03:55.280
A Marvel fan can dream.

